## Summary

This issue is for design discussions regarding a future move towards handling HDF5 data sets using a class.

## Requirements

### Data sets manage unique HDF5 handles

Like all objects in HDF5 data sets are managed through a handle of type `hid_t` which is returned by the library upon data set creation or opening. To avoid resource leaks these must be closed with a matching call to `H5Dclose` when done using the data set. Following RAII we should:

1. Manage this handle inside a class and release resources upon destruction
2. Not allow copies of this handle to exist: at most references
3. If possible not expose the handle outside of the class at all

Proposed base class:

```cpp
class DataSetBase
{
public:
    DataSetBase(hid_t handle) : handle_(handle) {}
    ~DataSetBase() noexcept
    {
        if (handle_ != H5I_INVALID_HID)
        {
            H5Dclose(handle_);
        }
    }
    DataSetBase(const DataSetBase&) = delete;            // disallow copy construction
    DataSetBase& operator=(const DataSetBase&) = delete; // disallow copy assignment
    DataSetBase(DataSetBase&& other)                     // move construction
    {
        std::swap(handle_, other.handle_);
    }
    DataSetBase& operator=(DataSetBase&& other)          // move assignment
    {
        std::swap(handle_, other.handle_);
        return *this;
    }

    // Get handle: preferably not needed!
    // hid_t id() const { return handle_; }

private:
    //! \brief Handle to open HDF5 data set.
    hid_t handle_ = H5I_INVALID_HID;
};
```

### Simple and compound data types

Data sets should be able to store:

* simple primitives `float`, `int32`, ...)
* strings:
  * fixed-length strings (e.g. for atom name)
  * variable-length strings (e.g. for provenance records)
* matrices `Matrix3x3` or `real[DIM][DIM]`)
* arrays of `RVec` for position, velocity and force: `real[N][DIM]` for `N` atoms

### Variable and static dimensions

Some data sets will store fixed-size data, such as lists of masses or charges for `N` atoms which will not change. Other data sets are for collecting per-frame data such as positions or observables.

* Fixed data sets
  * D-dimensional arrays of primitives (dimension: `[N]`, `[N][M]`, ...)
  * Arrays of (short) strings (e.g. atom names)
  * Single values (scalar data sets)?
* Variable (per-frame) data sets (`<variable>` denotes number of frames in the set, primary axis)
  * Primitives (dimension: `[<variable>]`)
  * Positions, velocities (dimension: `[<variable>][numAtoms][DIM]`)
  * Strings (provenance records)

### Type checking

The HDF5 library interface is untyped, working with generic handles and void pointers for reading and writing. As users we should always know what our data set represents, and thus never be able to try to read a string from a data set of integers.

Approach 1: Through inheritance

```cpp
template<typename ValueType>
class SimpleFramedDataSet : public DataSetBase
{
};

class RvecListFramedDataSet : public DataSetBase
{
};

// mockup signature for reading a frame from different data sets




void readFrame(SimpleFramedDataSet<float>& dataSet, int index, float& value);
void readFrame(RvecListFramedDataSet& dataSet, int index, ArrayRef<RVec> rvecList);
```

Approach 2: Associated type

```cpp
class RvecList;
class FixedSizeString;
class VariableSizeString;

template<typename DataType>
class DataSet { /* as above for DataSetBase */ };

// mockup signature for reading a frame from different data sets




void readFrame(DataSet<float>& dataSet, int index, float& value);
void readFrame(DataSet<RvecList>& dataSet, int index, ArrayRef<RVec> rvecList);
```

## Other considerations

### Bound methods for input/output

Free functions could be replaced with bound class methods. We can then keep all handles internal to the class and never expose them.

Problem: Data sets of different types have different requirements put on how to read and write data which is reflected in its function signature and body both: see the difference from reading a single `float` versus an `RVec`-array of all positions in a frame.

```cpp
template<typename ValueType>
class SimpleFramedDataSet : public DataSetBase
{
public:
    // ...
    void readValue(int index, ValueType& value);
    void writeValue(int index, const ValueType& value);
    // ...
};
```

```cpp
class RvecListFramedDataSet : public DataSetBase
{
public:
    // ...
    void readValue(int index, ArrayRef<RVec> value);
    void writeValue(int index, ArrayRef<const RVec> value);
    // ...
};
```

I don't think this is possible to do without inheritance into strongly typed classes?

### Manage data type in class

Data sets are created with a fixed type, used when writing or reading data from the set. A handle to this type could be easily managed.

```cpp
class DataSetBase
{
    // ... update destructor, move constructor and move assigner functions with data type handle
private:
    // ...
    //! \brief Open handle to the data type of the managed data set.
    hid_t dataType_;
};
```

### Reading or writing full data set in bulk

At least for simpler data types we may want to read all values at once, which would be dangerous for a data set containing trajectory data such as positions. Inheritance would solve this by only allowing this for simpler types.

```cpp
template<typename ValueType>
class SimpleFramedDataSet : public DataSetBase
{
public:
    // ...
    std::vector<ValueType> readAllValues() const;
    // ...
};
```

### Bounded versus unbounded frame data

Data sets which contain per-frame data, such as positions and energies, have an extra data set dimension for the frame indexing (always the major row, per H5md spec). We should consider the possibility of this being bounded or unbounded, and how to set this.

HDF5 data sets are always associated with a dimensionality (1d, 3d, etc.) and an internal data type which is separate from regular primitives such as `float` , `int32_t`, etc. Once a data set is created this cannot be changed. In return for being a bit more complicated, these internal data types handle a lot of tricky stuff when reading and writing data: for example it takes care of the endianness.

Since these types are internal to HDF5 we cannot let the compiler tell us when we are trying to write `float` values into a set that was created for `int32_t`, or even `float` into `double` values. We need to account for this ourselves. We currently check that types are compatible when reading or writing data from/to data sets, but a better strategy is desired.

1. At compile time we always know what types we want to read or write into a data set: our compiled structs are strongly typed.
2. Thus we can check for compatibility when opening data sets from existing trajectories
   1. (When creating sets we always know that the data set type is compatible with our compiled type)
   2. (But this is not the case when opening an already created set: it may have used `double` for positions whereas we are using `float`, or vice versa)
3. By checking these types at set opening/creation we know that the types match when reading or writing, and thus make this check a once-per-open operation instead of once-per-read/write.
4. Additionally, if something is wrong we fail and throw early instead of late which is always preferable

One implementation of this could be a `DataSet` class which contains the `hid_t` handles to the open data set, and possibly also one for its data type. Storing these together after asserting their compatibility means that the combination is secure to use.

Additionally, such a class could handle the set dimension, possibly with a strongly typed template: as with type we should always know the dimension our data has at compile time, although a possible complication could exist for future versions of the H5md specification. But by storing the data set dimension along with the handle and its type it's a lot easier to work with the sets.

Mock-up:

```cpp
class DataSet
{
private:
    int numDims_;    // number of dimensions of data set
    hid_t id_;       // handle to data set
    hid_t dataType_; // handle to data type

public:
    DataSet(hid_t dataSetId, int expectedNumDims) : id_{ dataSetId }
    {
        numDims_ =  // get from id_ (cannot change after set creation so can be stored)
        throwUponH5mdError(numDims_ != expectedNumDims, "Number of dimensions must match");
        dataType_ = H5Dget_type(id_); // ibid
    }

    hid_t id() const noexcept { return id_; }
    hid_t dataType() const noexcept { return dataType_; }
    std::vector<hsize_t> dims() const { /* compute current size along each axis */ }
    hsize_t numFrames() const { return dims()[0]; }
    // ...
};
```

Or with templated `numDims`:

```cpp
template<int numDims>
class DataSet
{
    DataSet(hid_t dataSetId) : id_{ dataSetId }
    {
        // check numDims and throw if not matching
        // ...
    }

    // our methods now always know the size to return which is nice!
    std::array<hsize_t, numDims> dims() const { /* ... */ }
};
```

Data set opening:

```cpp
// check compatibility at set opening time
template <typename ValueType>
DataSet openDataSet(hid_t container, std::string& dataSetName)
{
    hid_t dataSet = ... // open data set
    throwUponH5mdError(/* check the type against ValueType */, "Unexpected data type when opening data set.");
    return DataSet(dataSet);
}
```

Things to account for with a class:

* Stored handles (`hid_t` types) must not be closed by a user, and should possibly not be exposed at all (read/write functions could be methods)
* The destructor needs to close the opened handles (we could possibly use scope guards)
* We cannot have two `DataSets` with the same handles open, so we cannot allow copy construction/assignment
* Rule of five: Implement destructor and move constructor/assignment, delete copy construction/assignment
* 0-dimension data sets, will we need these? Subclass?

The following discussion from !4936 should be addressed:

- [ ] @mark.j.abraham started a [discussion](https://gitlab.com/gromacs/gromacs/-/merge_requests/4936#note_2350149988): (+2 comments)

  > If the number of dimensions is irrelevant to the frame count, then the caller shouldn't have to know or specify it in advance. Currently this uses `getDataSetDims<N>` in the implementation, but I think it should try to use an API call other than `H5Sget_simple_extent_dims`, because that one requires the caller know the number of dimensions. It can be implemented with `H5Sget_simple_extent_ndims` and a sufficiently large array with `H5Sget_simple_extent_dims` but that's not exactly elegant. I suggest we make an issue to revisit how this method works in practice once we have some more implementation done.
- [ ] @mark.j.abraham started a [discussion](https://gitlab.com/gromacs/gromacs/-/merge_requests/4936#note_2350149981): (+4 comments)

  > This would be a lot smoother with a strong type for `DataType<ValueType>` (containing the `hid_t`) because now the association between `ValueType` and the `hid_t` is set at the time we construct `DataType` (e.g. from a `hid_t` returned by some HDF5 API call). Now you don't need `DataType::matches()` method to do the job of this free function, because it can be compilation error to do anything with a mismatched combination of `DataType` and `ArrayRef<ValueType>`. Can you point me at a real use of this function?

  > If we retain this function, like `valueTypeToHdf5DataType` it would probably benefit from being in the header so that we avoid a lot of bouncing into tiny functions and verbosity from extern template declarations.

Refs #5016

The following discussion from !4506 should be addressed:

- [ ] @mark.j.abraham started a [discussion](https://gitlab.com/gromacs/gromacs/-/merge_requests/4506#note_2095944262):  (+14 comments)

    > It's potentially problematic to call open-or-create and either unilaterally close the group (which might double cross whatever already created the group and still wants it) or omit to close the group (leaking resources).
    > 
    > Life tends to flow better if you define the semantics of `gmx::H5md` to be as stateless as possible. That is, that where applicable our implementation will acquire resources it needs (such as opening a group) and release them wherever it is possible. Now it can be an error if the group is already open (throw gmx::APIError), which means we can find and fix other errors where we forgot to release resources. Adopting the laissez-faire approach is the path to wanting to use a language with a garbage collector, because nobody can know what is going on, which we aren't going to do :-).
    > 
    > That also means you can also have stuff like
    > ```
    > template<typename T>
    > class ScopeGuard
    > {
    >     private:
    >         T resource_;
    >         std::function<void(T)> deleter_;
    > 
    >     public:
    >         // Delete copy constructors
    >         // Implement move operations correctly
    >         ScopeGuard(T resource, std::function<void(T)>&& deleter) : resource_(resource), deleter_(std::move(deleter)) {}
    >         ~ScopeGuard() { deleter_(resource_); }
    >         T& resource() { return resource_; }
    >         const T& resource() { return resource_; }
    >         void release() { std::swap(deleter_, decltype(deleter_){}); }
    > };
    > 
    > using H5mdGroupGuard ScopeGuard<hid_t>;
    > 
    > std::string H5md::getCreatorProgramName()
    > {
    > #if GMX_USE_HDF5
    >     H5mdGroupGuard creatorGroup(openGroup(file_, "h5md/creator"), H5Gclose);
    >     // Blissfully forget about managing the lifetime of creatorGroup but only
    >     // if we know nobody else can care about it, thus don't call openOrCreateGroup
    > // ...
    > }
    > ```
