## Summary

Our `put_atoms_in_box` function uses `while` loops to wrap coordinates:

```cpp
while (x[i][m] >= box[m][m])
{
    for (d = 0; d <= m; d++) 
    {
        x[i][d] -= box[m][d];
        if constexpr (haveBoxDeformation)
        {
            v[i][d] -= boxDeformation[m][d];
        }
    }
}
```

That is fine, unless one of the coordinates becomes infinite, and the loop never breaks.

## Exact steps to reproduce

```cpp
TEST(PbcTest, PutAtonsInBoxHandlesNan)
{
    const matrix           box = { { 3.5, 0, 0 }, { 0, 3.5, 0 }, { 0, 0, 3.5 } };
    const real badValue = std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();
    std::vector<gmx::RVec> points{ { badValue, badValue, badValue } };
    put_atoms_in_box(PbcType::Xyz, box, points);
}
```

This hangs instead of crashing or returning garbage (the latter is fine too).

## For developers: Why is this important?

Not hugely important since physics is already violated, but an infinite loop instead of a crash is very confusing and wasteful.

## Possible fixes

- `std::trunc` / `std::fmod` instead of loops?
- `std::infinite` checks?

Performance needs to be assessed, which makes this issue complicated.

## Summary

Hi all. I'm new to gitlab, so my account history is blank, but I've been using gromacs for roughly four years and am active on github. I'm opening an issue about `gmx chi` and also sort of using this as a petition to not deprecate the tool as discussed in #4108. This tool has conveniences compared to tools like `gmx angle` which personally have saved me a lot of time.

<ins>Please summarise the issue or bug. What exact version of the code did it happen with? Was that a vanilla or modified version?</ins>

I'm using vanilla gromacs and this behavior is present in the current version of gromacs. I'm using gmx chi to generate distributions of the side chain torsional angles of various amino acids in short peptides. In the case of amino acids with 1 side chain torsion angle, the -rama option does not produce anything. This is apparently by design. From `gmx_chi.cpp`: 

```
if (has_dihedral(edChi1, dihedral) && has_dihedral(edChi2, dihedral))
        {
            sprintf(fn, "ramaX1X2%s.xvg", dihedral.name);
            fp  = rama_file(fn,
                           "\\8c\\4\\s1\\N-\\8c\\4\\s2\\N Ramachandran Plot",
                           "\\8c\\4\\s1\\N (deg)",
                           "\\8c\\4\\s2\\N (deg)",
                           oenv);
            Xi1 = dihedral.j0[edChi1];
            Xi2 = dihedral.j0[edChi2];
            for (int j = 0; (j < nf); j++)
            {
                fprintf(fp, "%10g  %10g\n", gmx::c_rad2Deg * dih[Xi1][j], gmx::c_rad2Deg * dih[Xi2][j]);
            }
            xvgrclose(fp);
        }
        else
        {
            fprintf(stderr, "No chi1 & chi2 angle for %s\n", dihedral.name);
        }
```

So, that means to get the chi 1 angle as a function of time for valine, as an example, I'd have to use the -all option. The -all flag separately produces a file for each torsion angle of each residue separately. If the system I'm looking at gets reasonably large, the number of output files exponentially grows. The only real control for this is to specify a starting residue. But, if I care about a valine that is residue 2 of a 100 residue protein, I produce over 200 files to get the necessary information (and this ignores other residues with less than 2 side chain torsion angles throughout the rest of the protein that I might be interested in). So it's likely that I'm producing a lot of data that I will just delete, which is inefficient.

The obvious solution is to default to `gmx angle`. However, if I do care about multiple residues or multiple dihedral/torsion angles within each residue, I have to manually make an index file with each 4-atom dihedral in a separate group (unless there's a specific way to automate that I'm unaware of) and run the `angle` command potentially multiple times. So, `angle` has the flexibility, but `chi` has the convenience of detecting the angles that are generally of interest (phi/psi/chi1/chi2/etc.) and computing them in one command.

So, my essential problem statement is that `gmx chi` is a very convenient tool but it produces way too many unnecessary files and can be a little inflexible. A couple of small changes could help the tool be a little more flexible and user-friendly with regards to outputs. I propose a couple ideas below for discussion.

<ins>If this refers to a more general issue or feature, is it important to fix right now, and would you have time to help?</ins>

This is probably very low priority considering the tool is being considered for deprecation. I would be interested in helping apply changes. I'm opening an issue first to discuss before dedicating the time but would be happy to fork, make changes, and make a pull request for changes based on the result of the discussion, if applicable.

## Exact steps to reproduce

<ins>How one can reproduce the issue - this is important; remember to attach all relevant files, in particular the original files so we can regenerate the TPR when necessary.</ins>

I can produce files if necessary, but the quoted source code above should demonstrate my point. I've also posted about this in the forum a while ago: https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/missing-dihedral-recognition-using-gmx-chi/5262

## Possible fixes

I have three suggestions but I'm suggesting them as individual "solutions". I am not suggesting to implement all of them. 

1. This is the simplest. Add an option to specify the last residue. Say `-r0` for first residue and `-rN` for last. Now, the user can use the chi tool for a shorter segment of the protein in the system. And, if the user specifies the same residue for both (`-r0 i -rN i`) in conjunction with `-all`, the user just gets output for that individual residue, filling in gaps for residues not captured in `-rama`. Or, the user has a series of short short side chains in a sequence and could use the `-all` option just on that part of the sequence. This would greatly reduce the amount of extraneous output to analyze part of the system and reduce time making manual index files for larger systems to use `gmx angle` as a replacement.
2. Separate the calculation of backbone and side chain angles (i.e. separate `-rama` to `-rama` and `-chi`). That way, if the user requires the use of `-all` for some residues and `-rama` for other residues, they are not reproducing the backbone distributions for all the residues with less than or greater than 2 side chain torsions.
3. When using `-rama`, rather than using `if` blocks to specify specific conditions (i.e. if two chi angles are present as in the above code snippet), the output for side chain torsions could just include a column for each chi angle present in the residue and the name of the file could be appended accordingly (i.e. ramaX1VAL.xvg and ramaX1X2..X5ARG.xvg). Then the number of columns output could be controlled via `-maxchi` by the user (so something like ramaX1X2ARG.xvg is still a possible output). This way, the user has more freedom about which data is generated from the commands, and the decision to use -all or -rama, or the need to use both, is not dictated by the sequence of the protein. Additionally, looking into residues with many chi angles does not require generating multiple individual files for every residue in the sequence but only extra (or fewer) columns within each side chain output file for a residue. Additionally, if implemented in conjunction with point 1, there could also be fewer files in general because I could pick specific residues.

Personally, I like the simplicity of option 1 but think option 3 would be the most complete "solution" while balancing the number of required changes. Either option allows more control over the output and allows for the user to reduce the production of extraneous data. Option 2 requires the introduction of an entirely new function so is less desirable. Overall, though, `gmx chi` is more convenient to use than others for various reasons such as automatically detecting angles of interest over amino acid residues of an entire or large portions of a protein. While `gmx rama` does this for backbones, there would not be an equivalent for side chain torsions without the availability of `gmx chi` (short of using another package, which has it's own drawbacks). 

Thanks for taking the time to read my note. I apologize for its length, but I hope it shows thought went into it. 

Best,

Brian

