`acc-grps` and `accelerate` are deprecated in the 2021 release and will be removed in the 2022 one. This may cause a few problems to all users performing simulations of confined micro/nanoflows, as `accelerate` is by far the easiest way to obtain a Poiseuille (parabolic) flow profile, which is important for numerous applications, being it equivalent to a pressure driven flow. The other options to achieve the same results are either a overkill (pull, AWH) or very unpractical (solid piston).

A quick search on the GROMACS Bioexcel forum (https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/) reveals how many users are either using or planning to use (or even worse: being suggested to use) `accelerate` for these kind of simualtions:
https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/constant-acceleration-md-anti-bug/1614/2 ;
https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/grompp-error-invalid-acceleration-input-2-groups-and-2-acc-values/2666 ;
https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/how-to-apply-com-pull-on-molecules-of-the-same-type/2945/2 ;
https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/graphene-wall-piston/1601 ;
https://gromacs.bioexcel.eu/t/is-it-possbile-and-how-to-apply-an-directional-hydrostatic-pressure-by-the-pull-code/2796

I would like to point out that one of the reasons for deleting the feature is that 'nobody uses it' (https://gitlab.com/gromacs/gromacs/-/issues/1354).

I personally believe `accelerate` not only is useful (and used), but it also need to be 'revived' with some additional feature; in particular:
- the possibility of **biasing** the temperature calculation with linear and parabolic velocity profiles, in such a way that thermal velocity (the one defining temperature) is distingushed from the peculiar velocity (the one due to the hydrodinamic flow) [1], [2]
- the possibility to **confine** the application of the acceleration field in a section of space, so that one can emulate a _fluidic piston_, which in turns is useful to assess flows close to solid walls where fluid density is not uniform [3]

[1] J. H. Ferzinger, H. G. Kaper, Mathematical theory of transport processes in gases (book);
[2] S. Bernardi, B. D. Todd, D. J. Searles, Thermostating highly confined fluids, J. Chem. Phys. 132, 244706 (2010);
[3] C. Herrero, T. Omori, Y. Yamaguchi, L. Joly, Shear force measurement of the hydrodynamic wall position in molecular dynamics, J. Chem. Phys. 151, 041103 (2019)

